# Frontend Architecture & AI Coding Standards

Mirror for Claude: **`.claude/agents/frontend-architecture.md`**.

Portal Page file naming, SCSS modules, BEM, `app-context` / `constants.ts`, and `index.html` globals: **`.cursor/rules/code-style.mdc`**.

## Purpose

You are working on an **existing** React + TypeScript application (Portal Page / Custom App with pp-dev).

Produce **consistent, maintainable, reusable, component-driven** code that follows the existing architecture and design system.

Do **not** treat each task as an isolated implementation. Before writing code: understand structure, find reusable components/patterns, follow conventions already in the repo.

Evolve a **consistent component system**, not a collection of independent implementations.

---

## 1. Explicit component hierarchy

```text
Application
  └── Pages
        └── Widgets
              └── Features
                    └── Entities
                          └── Shared
                                └── Design System
                                      └── @metricinsights/pp-components
```

Map new work to this hierarchy. When the repo folder layout differs (e.g. `src/components/<feature>/`), place code in the **closest existing layer** and keep responsibilities clear — do not invent a parallel tree without need.

### Design system

Lowest-level UI: Button, Input, Select, Checkbox, Radio, Modal, Drawer, Tooltip, Dropdown, Table, Card, Typography, Icon, etc. from **`@metricinsights/pp-components`** (and project wrappers). Prefer these; do not recreate existing primitives.

### Shared

Reusable across features: PageHeader, PageToolbar, SearchBar, FilterBar, EmptyState, LoadingState, Pagination. Only if genuinely multi-feature.

### Entities

Domain concepts: User, Folder, Element, Company, Report, Announcement. Keep entity UI/logic close to the entity when appropriate.

### Features

User actions / business functionality: CreateUser, EditFolder, FavoriteElement, SearchElements, RequestAccess. Keep feature-specific UI here — not in global shared.

### Widgets

Compose features/components into larger sections: Sidebar, FavoriteBar, ElementBrowser, SearchPanel, AnnouncementPanel. Compose; do not reimplement everything.

### Pages

Compose widgets, features, and shared. Prefer thin pages:

```tsx
export function AccessRequestsPage() {
  return (
    <>
      <PageHeader />
      <AccessRequestFilters />
      <AccessRequestTable />
    </>
  );
}
```

Do not dump hundreds of lines of JSX into a page.

---

## 2. Component-driven development

Before creating a component: search the repo → search the design system → find similar implementations → reuse or extend via props → create new only if nothing fits.

One clear responsibility. Split when too large or mixed concerns. Do **not** extract every small JSX fragment — extract when it improves reuse, readability, or separation.

---

## 3. Search before writing

**Before writing new code, inspect the repository.**

Do not assume a component, hook, utility, type, constant, helper, style pattern, or API abstraction is missing. Search first.

When a similar solution exists: **follow that pattern** — consistency beats a “cleaner” new approach.

Examples of what **not** to do when patterns already exist:

- Introducing `div` + ad-hoc flex classes when the project uses layout primitives
- Custom `<button className="…">` when `<Button variant="primary">` exists
- Raw `fetch` / `axios` when `useSWRUser()` (or other established SWR hooks) already wrap the API

---

## 4. Design system first

Default to design-system / pp-components for buttons, inputs, forms, selects, dropdowns, modals, drawers, tooltips, tables, cards, typography, icons, loading/empty states, navigation, layout primitives.

Verify no existing primitive can satisfy the need before creating a new one.

---

## 5. Styling

Follow **`.cursor/rules/code-style.mdc`** (SCSS modules + BEM + tokens).

Do not invent colors, font sizes/weights, spacing, radii, shadows, breakpoints, z-index, or animation timings when design-system / `_variables` / mixins exist.

No new CSS framework or styling methodology without explicit approval. Do not mix approaches unnecessarily.

---

## 6. JavaScript / TypeScript whitespace and formatting

### Source of truth

Apply formatting rules in the following order of precedence:

1. Repository ESLint, Prettier, TypeScript, and formatter configuration.
2. Existing conventions in the current file and surrounding code.
3. Google JavaScript Style Guide — Formatting / Whitespace, as a non-binding fallback:
   https://google.github.io/styleguide/jsguide.html#formatting-whitespace

The Google guide must not override repository tooling or established local conventions. It is not the project's complete coding-style specification.

### Repository formatting rules

Unless a file-specific override applies:

- End every file with a newline.
- Limit lines to **120 characters**.
- Limit each line to at most **3 statements**.
- Use curly braces for all control-flow bodies, including single-statement bodies.
- Format content inside curly braces according to the configured `@stylistic/curly-newline` rule.
- Do not place whitespace before an object property colon.
- Place exactly one space after an object property colon.
- Separate class members with a blank line.
- A blank line is optional after a single-line class member.

### Blank lines between statements

Apply these rules:

- Consecutive `const`, `let`, and `var` declarations may remain together without blank lines.
- Add a blank line after the final declaration in a declaration group when the next statement is not another declaration.
- Add a blank line after a block statement.
- Add a blank line after `if`, `for`, `while`, `switch`, and `try` statements.
- Add a blank line before:
  - `return`;
  - `throw`;
  - `if`;
  - `for`;
  - `while`;
  - `switch`;
  - `try`.
- Consecutive expression statements do not require blank lines.

### Exceptions

Respect all file-specific ESLint and formatter overrides.

For example, the **120-character line limit does not apply** to:

```text
src/components/icon/icons/**/*.tsx
```

This exception exists because inline SVG exports may contain long path definitions.

### Rules for generated or modified code

AI agents and developers must:

- use repository formatting and lint commands when available;
- produce formatter- and lint-compatible code;
- preserve the formatting conventions of the current file;
- avoid unrelated whitespace or formatting changes;
- avoid manually reformatting code against configured tooling;
- preserve file-specific overrides and generated-code exceptions;
- keep formatting-only changes separate from behavioral changes when practical.

If tooling and documentation disagree, tooling takes precedence.

This section covers formatting only. All other repository lint rules—including naming, type safety, React Hooks, console usage, and class organization—remain mandatory and must pass the configured lint checks.

